In TypeScript, you can use type assertions or type guards to inform the type system that you know the type of a value at a certain point in your code. However, if you want to assert that a value will only have one specific type in practice, you can use a type assertion with a type guard to narrow down the type.

Here's an example where you might have a value that could be of multiple types, but you know it will only be one type in practice:

```typescript
function processValue(value: string | number | boolean): void {
    if (typeof value === 'string') {
        // value is known to be a string in this block
        console.log(`Processing string: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'number') {
        // value is known to be a number in this block
        console.log(`Processing number: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'boolean') {
        // value is known to be a boolean in this block
        console.log(`Processing boolean: ${value}`);
    }
}

// Example usage
processValue("hello"); // This will log "Processing string: hello"
processValue(42);      // This will log "Processing number: 42"
processValue(true);    // This will log "Processing boolean: true"
```

If you want to assert that a value will only be one specific type in practice, you can use a type assertion within a conditional check. For example:

```typescript
