In TypeScript, you can use type assertions or type guards to inform the type system that you know the type of a value at a certain point in your code. However, if you want to assert that a value will only have one specific type in practice, you can use a type assertion with a type guard to narrow down the type.

Here's an example where you might have a value that could be of multiple types, but you know it will only be one type in practice:

```typescript
function processValue(value: string | number | boolean): void {
    if (typeof value === 'string') {
        // value is known to be a string in this block
        console.log(`Processing string: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'number') {
        // value is known to be a number in this block
        console.log(`Processing number: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'boolean') {
        // value is known to be a boolean in this block
        console.log(`Processing boolean: ${value}`);
    }
}

// Example usage
processValue("hello"); // This will be treated as a string
processValue(42);      // This will be treated as a number
processValue(true);    // This will be treated as a boolean
```

If you want to assert that a value will only be one specific type in practice, you can use a type assertion:

```typescript
function processValue(value: string | number | boolean): void {
    if (typeof value === 'string') {
        // value is known to be a string in this block
        console.log(`Processing string: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'number') {
        // value is known to be a number in this block
        console.log(`Processing number: ${value}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'boolean') {
        // value is known to be a boolean in this block
        console.log(`Processing boolean: ${value}`);
    }

    // Type assertion to narrow down the type
    if (typeof value === 'string') {
        const strValue: string = value; // Type assertion
        console.log(`Str value: ${strValue}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'number') {
        const numValue: number = value; // Type assertion
        console.log(`Num value: ${numValue}`);
    } else if (typeof value === 'boolean') {
        const boolValue: boolean = value; // Type assertion
        console.log(`Bool value: ${boolValue}`);
    }
}

// Example usage
processValue("hello"); // This will be treated as a string
processValue(42);      // This will be treated as a number
processValue(true);    // This will be treated as a boolean
```

In this example, the type assertions (`const strValue: string = value;`, `const numValue: number = value;`, `const boolValue: boolean = value;`) are used to inform the type system that you know the value will be of a specific type within the block where the assertion is made. This can help the type checker understand the type of the value in that specific context.

